, "level": 1, "posterior": 0.07083039428751423, "danger": 0.9, "evidence_for": ["35% blasts in a subacute context with pancytopenia strongly favors acute myeloid processes (e.g., AML) over chronic myeloproliferative disorders in blast crisis, which typically present with a history of chronic disease and splenomegaly.", "The 35% blast count exceeds typical thresholds for ALL (15-20%), but absence of lymphoid morphology and atypical neurological findings (ataxia, visual decline) reduce lymphoid process likelihood.", "Blast crisis of CML/polycythemia vera typically follows chronic phases with splenomegaly; absence of these features makes this less likely but not excluded in the context of rapid transformation.", "Visual acuity decline and ataxia are not typical of ALL, reducing its likelihood. These findings could represent paraneoplastic phenomena in AML but don't exclude blast crisis.", "Normal creatinine and absence of splenomegaly are inconsistent with chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms (e.g., CML, primary myelofibrosis) in accelerated/blast crisis phases, which often cause renal dysfunction and splenomegaly."], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Does the patient have a known history of chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm?", "Are there signs of marrow failure (e.g., thrombocytopenia, anemia)?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Bone marrow biopsy with cytogenetics", "Flow cytometry for blast lineage", "BCR-ABL1 fusion gene testing"]}, "target_level": 2}
